      "math_academy_id": "e-49",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\sqrt{x-2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)= \\sqrt{x-2}$. Notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =\\sqrt{2-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the graph of $y=f(x)$ \"starts\" at $x=2.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253135/assets/a4a64caff7a8b69454ffc8b93a1baad18bf3d93093b3c85a7c4fc3cb9762a2b4.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we notice that the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = \\textrm{DNE},\n$$\n\nwhile the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} f(x)= \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$",
